ENFORCEMENT DECREE OF THE ACT ON CARBON DIOXIDE CAPTURE, TRANSPORT, STORAGE AND UTILIZATION

CHAPTER I GENERAL PROVISIONS

Article 1 (Purpose) The purpose of this Decree is to prescribe matters delegated by the Act on Carbon Dioxide Capture, Transport, Storage and Utilization and matters necessary for its enforcement.

Article 2 (Scope of Transport)
① “Carbon dioxide prescribed by Presidential Decree” in subparagraph 2 of Article 2 of the Act on Carbon Dioxide Capture, Transport, Storage and Utilization (hereinafter referred to as the “Act”) means carbon dioxide falling under any of the following subparagraphs that exists in a gaseous, liquid or solid state [including a supercritical fluid (a state having properties of both a gas and a liquid)]:
1. Carbon dioxide generated from power generation facilities, manufacturing processes and other industrial activities;
2. Carbon dioxide generated in the process of capturing, transporting, storing or utilizing carbon dioxide;
3. Carbon dioxide in the atmosphere.
② “Means prescribed by Presidential Decree” in subparagraph 2 of Article 2 of the Act means any of the following means: <Amended on October 1, 2025>
1. A carbon dioxide transportation pipeline;
2. A vessel under Article 1-2 (1) of the Ship Act (hereinafter referred to as a “vessel”);
3. A freight motor vehicle under subparagraph 1 of Article 2 of the Trucking Transport Business Act (hereinafter referred to as a “freight motor vehicle”);
4. A railroad vehicle under subparagraph 4 of Article 3 of the Framework Act on the Development of Railroad Industry (hereinafter referred to as a “railroad vehicle”);
5. Any other means publicly notified by the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment upon determining that it is capable of transporting carbon dioxide safely.

Article 3 (Scope of Carbon Dioxide Transportation Pipelines) “Facilities prescribed by Presidential Decree” in subparagraph 5 of Article 2 of the Act means the following facilities; provided that carbon dioxide transportation pipes and related facilities installed on vessels are excluded: <Amended on October 1, 2025>
1. Carbon dioxide transportation pipes (meaning main pipes and auxiliary pipes extending from capture facilities to storage sites or facilities for utilization business; hereinafter the same shall apply);
2. Ancillary equipment, including valves and emergency shutoff devices, installed on the carbon dioxide transportation pipes under subparagraph 1;
3. Other related facilities necessary to convey captured carbon dioxide to a storage site or a facility for utilization business, as prescribed and publicly notified by the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment.

Article 4 (Criteria for Storage Sites)
① “Place meeting the criteria prescribed by Presidential Decree” in subparagraph 6 of Article 2 of the Act means a place that meets all of the following requirements:
1. It shall have geological characteristics that allow stable injection and storage of carbon dioxide;
2. There shall be no risk of a major leakage due to environmental changes, such as earthquakes or volcanic eruptions.
② Detailed criteria for storage sites under paragraph ① shall be prescribed and publicly notified by the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment. <Amended on October 1, 2025>

CHAPTER II ESTABLISHMENT OF MASTER PLANS FOR CAPTURE AND RELATED ACTIVITIES

Article 5 (Establishment of Master Plans for Capture and Related Activities)
① For the efficient establishment of a master plan concerning carbon dioxide capture, transport, storage and utilization under Article 5 (1) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as “capture and related activities” and the “master plan,” respectively), the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment shall formulate guidelines for preparing a draft master plan after consulting with the Minister of Science and ICT and notify the heads of the relevant central administrative agencies thereof. <Amended on October 1, 2025>
② The head of each relevant central administrative agency shall prepare a draft master plan on matters under his or her jurisdiction in accordance with the guidelines under paragraph ① and submit it to the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment. The Minister shall prepare an integrated draft master plan, consult with the Minister of Science and ICT and the heads of relevant central administrative agencies, and finalize the master plan in accordance with the procedure under Article 5 (3) of the Act. <Amended on October 1, 2025>
③ If the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment intends to change any matter specified in a subparagraph of Article 6 (2) in the master plan finalized under paragraph ②, the Minister shall prepare a proposed amendment, consult with the Minister of Science and ICT and the heads of relevant central administrative agencies, and submit it to the National Committee on Climate Crisis Response under Article 15 of the Framework Act on Carbon Neutrality and Green Growth for Coping with Climate Crisis (hereinafter referred to as the “National Committee on Climate Crisis Response”). <Amended on October 1, 2025; March 31, 2026>
④ When a master plan is finalized or changed under paragraph ② or ③, the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment shall notify the heads of relevant central administrative agencies thereof. <Amended on October 1, 2025>

Article 6 (Contents of Master Plans for Capture and Related Activities)
① “Matters prescribed by Presidential Decree” in Article 5 (2) 10 of the Act means the following matters:
1. Establishment and improvement of a system for reducing carbon dioxide through capture and related activities;
2. Training of specialized personnel for technology development concerning capture and related activities;
3. Standardization of technologies concerning capture and related activities;
4. Measures for international cooperation and overseas expansion concerning capture and related activities.
② “Important matters prescribed by Presidential Decree” in the latter part of Article 5 (3) of the Act means any of the following matters:
1. Matters concerning the basic direction and objectives of capture and related activities;
2. Matters concerning the selection and management of candidate carbon dioxide storage sites under Article 14 of the Act;
3. Matters concerning safety management and monitoring of storage sites.

Article 7 (Establishment of Implementation Plans for Capture and Related Activities)
① When the head of a relevant central administrative agency establishes an implementation plan concerning carbon dioxide capture and related activities under Article 6 (1) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as an “implementation plan”), he or she shall submit the plan, together with the preceding year's implementation results, to the National Committee on Climate Crisis Response by March 31 of each year. <Amended on March 31, 2026>
② When establishing an implementation plan, the head of a relevant central administrative agency shall ensure that it is linked to the master plan.

CHAPTER III INSTALLATION OF CARBON DIOXIDE CAPTURE AND TRANSPORT FACILITIES

Article 8 (Reporting Plans to Install Carbon Dioxide Capture Facilities)
① A person intending to install and operate carbon dioxide capture facilities under Article 7 (1) of the Act shall submit to the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment a report in the form prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Climate, Energy and Environment, accompanied by a plan to install and operate carbon dioxide capture facilities under the same paragraph (hereinafter referred to as an “installation plan”). <Amended on October 1, 2025>
② “Matters prescribed by Presidential Decree” in Article 7 (2) 4 of the Act means the following matters:
1. Construction design documents, a construction schedule and safety measures for carbon dioxide capture facilities;
2. Costs and duration of installing carbon dioxide capture facilities;
3. A financing plan for the costs of installing carbon dioxide capture facilities;
4. An annual maintenance and management plan for carbon dioxide capture facilities;
5. A safety management plan for carbon dioxide capture facilities;
6. A plan for treating, storing and utilizing captured carbon dioxide.
③ “Important matters prescribed by Presidential Decree” in Article 7 (3) of the Act means matters concerning the site of installation, including the location and area of carbon dioxide capture facilities, and matters concerning equipment, including its capacity and method of capture.
④ A person intending to report a change to an installation plan under Article 7 (3) of the Act shall submit to the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment a change report in the form prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Climate, Energy and Environment, accompanied by documents verifying the change, at least 30 days before changing the installation plan. <Amended on October 1, 2025>
⑤ Upon receiving a report under paragraph ①, the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment shall verify the following matters through the shared use of administrative information under Article 36 (1) of the Electronic Government Act; provided that, if the reporting person does not consent to verification of the certificate of business registration, the Minister shall require that person to attach that document: <Amended on October 1, 2025>
1. Certificate of business registration;
2. Certificate of registered corporate matters (only where the reporting person is a corporation).
⑥ If the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment intends to consult with the heads of relevant central administrative agencies under Article 7 (4) of the Act, the Minister shall send them a copy of the report under paragraph ① or the change report under paragraph ④. <Amended on October 1, 2025>
⑦ The head of a relevant central administrative agency who receives a request for consultation under paragraph ⑥ shall submit his or her opinion to the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment within 30 days from the date of receipt of the request. <Amended on October 1, 2025>

Article 9 (Support for Capture Business Operators) Within the budget, the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment may support the facility and operating funds for carbon dioxide capture facilities of a person who has reported the installation and operation of such facilities under Article 7 (1) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as a “capture business operator”). <Amended on October 1, 2025>

Article 10 (Application for Approval of Carbon Dioxide Transportation Business)
① “Facilities and documents prescribed by Presidential Decree” in the main clause of Article 8 (1) of the Act means the following facilities and documents for each means of transport: <Amended on October 1, 2025>
1. Transport by carbon dioxide transportation pipeline:
  (a) Pipes, control and monitoring facilities, firefighting facilities, equipment for protecting human life, and other facilities prescribed and publicly notified by the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment for transporting captured carbon dioxide;
  (b) Documents proving ownership of the carbon dioxide transportation pipeline or the right to use and manage it;
2. Transport by vessel:
  (a) A carrier vessel, temporary storage equipment, loading and unloading equipment, firefighting facilities, equipment for protecting human life, and other facilities prescribed and publicly notified by the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment for transporting captured carbon dioxide;
  (b) A copy of the certificate of registration for marine cargo transport business issued under Article 24 of the Marine Transportation Act;
3. Transport by freight motor vehicle:
  (a) A storage tank, emergency shutoff device, and other facilities prescribed and publicly notified by the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment for transporting captured carbon dioxide;
  (b) A copy of the permit for general freight motor vehicle transport business issued under Article 3 of the Trucking Transport Business Act and a copy of the high-pressure gas carrier registration certificate issued under Article 5-4 of the High-Pressure Gas Safety Control Act;
4. Transport by railroad vehicle:
  (a) A tank fixed to the railroad vehicle, an emergency shutoff device, and other facilities prescribed and publicly notified by the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment for transporting captured carbon dioxide;
  (b) A copy of the railroad business license issued under Article 5 of the Railroad Business Act.
② A person intending to obtain approval for a business transporting captured carbon dioxide under the main clause of Article 8 (1) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as a “transportation business”) shall submit an application for approval in the form prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Climate, Energy and Environment to the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment, accompanied by the following documents: <Amended on October 1, 2025>
1. Business plan;
2. Articles of incorporation or bylaws;
3. Documents specified for the relevant means of transport in the subparagraphs of paragraph ① (including documents proving that the facilities for that means of transport are in place).
③ Upon receiving an application under paragraph ②, the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment shall verify the following matters through the shared use of administrative information under Article 36 (1) of the Electronic Government Act; provided that, if the applicant does not consent to verification of the certificate of business registration, the Minister shall require the applicant to attach that document: <Amended on October 1, 2025>
1. Certificate of business registration;
2. Certificate of registered corporate matters (only if the applicant is a corporation).
④ Matters necessary for approval of a transportation business, other than those prescribed in paragraphs ① through ③, shall be prescribed and publicly notified by the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment. <Amended on October 1, 2025>

Article 11 (Inspection of Compliance with Safety Management Rules)
① Under Article 9 (3) of the Act, the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment shall inspect whether a pipeline installer and operator (meaning a person approved under paragraph (1) of the same Article; hereinafter the same shall apply) and his or her personnel comply with the safety management rules (meaning the rules under paragraph (1) of the same Article; hereinafter the same shall apply) within 30 days before or after the date falling six months after approval of the transportation business. Thereafter, the Minister shall conduct periodic inspections within 30 days before or after each date falling five years after the first inspection, calculated from the date of that inspection. <Amended on October 1, 2025>
② The criteria for inspecting compliance with safety management rules and other necessary matters shall be prescribed and publicly notified by the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment. <Amended on October 1, 2025>

Article 12 (Qualifications of Safety Managers)
① Safety managers whom a pipeline installer and operator must appoint under Article 10 (1) of the Act are classified as persons responsible for safety management and safety management personnel.
② A person responsible for safety management shall directly manage the carbon dioxide transportation pipeline and direct and supervise safety management personnel. Safety management personnel shall perform safety management duties under the direction of the person responsible for safety management.
③ The qualifications and number of safety managers for each category of persons subject to appointment under paragraph ① shall be as specified in attached Table 1.

Article 13 (Scope of Duties of Safety Managers)
① A safety manager shall perform the following safety management duties:
1. Maintaining the safety of the carbon dioxide transportation pipeline in accordance with the safety management rules, and preparing and retaining records thereof;
2. Preparing and retaining records of safety inspections under Article 11 (1) of the Act;
3. Providing safety training to personnel at the place of business;
4. Taking other preventive measures for the safety of the carbon dioxide transportation pipeline and the prevention of hazards.
② A safety manager shall not be assigned duties other than those specified in the subparagraphs of paragraph ①.
③ If a safety manager appointed under Article 10 (1) of the Act falls under any subparagraph of paragraph (3) of the same Article, the pipeline installer and operator shall designate a substitute to perform that manager's duties for the following period, as applicable:
1. Where Article 10 (3) 1 of the Act applies: the period during which the manager cannot perform the duties, not exceeding 30 days;
2. Where Article 10 (3) 2 of the Act applies: until another safety manager is appointed.
④ A substitute designated to perform a safety manager's duties under Article 10 (3) of the Act and paragraph ③ of this Article shall be a person specified below, as applicable:
1. For the duties of a person responsible for safety management: a member of the safety management personnel;
2. For the duties of a member of the safety management personnel: a person employed at the relevant place of business who works on the installation or operation of carbon dioxide transportation pipelines and has knowledge of pipeline safety management.

Article 14 (Conduct of Safety Inspections)
① A person intending to undergo a safety inspection under Article 11 (1) of the Act shall submit an application for safety inspection in the form prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Climate, Energy and Environment to the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment. <Amended on October 1, 2025>
② The period for regular inspection under Article 11 (1) 1 of the Act is within 30 days before or after the expiry date of the inspection's validity (or the extended expiry date if the validity period has been extended or the inspection deferred under Article 11 (3) of the Act). An inspection undergone within this period shall be deemed to have been undergone on the expiry date of the validity period.
③ The Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment shall promptly notify a person who fails a safety inspection of the reasons for failure and the matters requiring improvement. <Amended on October 1, 2025>
④ A person notified of failure under paragraph ③ shall improve the nonconforming matters and undergo reinspection within two months after receiving the notice of failure; provided that, if an unavoidable reason exists, such as the time needed for technical review, that period may be extended with the approval of the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment. <Amended on October 1, 2025>

Article 15 (Extension of Validity Period of Safety Inspections)
① The Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment may extend the validity period of a safety inspection or defer its conduct under Article 11 (3) of the Act as follows: <Amended on October 1, 2025>
1. If an inspection cannot be undergone within its validity period due to maintenance of a carbon dioxide transportation pipeline or similar reasons: extension of the validity period of the safety inspection;
2. If a natural disaster, war, disturbance or comparable emergency occurs: deferral of the safety inspection.
② If a pipeline installer and operator is deemed unable to undergo a safety inspection within its validity period for the reason under paragraph ① 1, the operator shall submit to the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment an application to extend the validity period of the safety inspection in the form prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Climate, Energy and Environment, accompanied by documents proving the need for extension, before that period expires. <Amended on October 1, 2025>
③ If the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment defers a safety inspection under paragraph ① 2, the Minister shall promptly give public notice of the period of deferral and the areas covered. <Amended on October 1, 2025>

CHAPTER IV EXPLORATION, SELECTION AND CLOSURE OF CANDIDATE CARBON DIOXIDE STORAGE SITES

Article 16 (Procedures for Approval of Exploration)
① A person seeking approval under Article 13 (1) of the Act to explore for a storage site underground on land or under the sea shall submit an application for approval of exploration in the form prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Climate, Energy and Environment to the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment, accompanied by the documents specified in the subparagraphs of Article 13 (2) of the Act. <Amended on October 1, 2025>
② “Documents prescribed by Presidential Decree” in Article 13 (2) 5 of the Act means the following documents: <Amended on October 1, 2025>
1. Documents concerning the capability to perform the business, including technical personnel and a financing plan;
2. Other documents prescribed and publicly notified by the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment as necessary.
③ Upon receiving an application under paragraph ①, the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment shall verify the following matters through the shared use of administrative information under Article 36 (1) of the Electronic Government Act; provided that, if the applicant does not consent to verification of the certificate of business registration, the Minister shall require the applicant to attach that document: <Amended on October 1, 2025>
1. Certificate of business registration;
2. Certificate of registered corporate matters (only if the applicant is a corporation).
④ The Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment shall review the documents submitted with an application under paragraph ① and may request the applicant for exploration approval to supplement them within a specified period if their contents are unclear, omitted or otherwise require supplementation. <Amended on October 1, 2025>
⑤ If, after reviewing an application for exploration approval submitted under paragraph ①, the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment determines that the criteria for approval under Article 17 have been met, the Minister shall issue a certificate of exploration approval in the form prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Climate, Energy and Environment. <Amended on October 1, 2025>

Article 17 (Criteria for Approval of Exploration)
① “Criteria for approval of exploration prescribed by Presidential Decree” in the former part of Article 13 (3) of the Act means the following criteria:
1. The applicant shall have the technical and financial capability necessary to conduct exploration;
2. The exploration shall not significantly obstruct another person's exercise of a right, including an exploration right under Article 12 of the Submarine Mineral Resources Development Act, in the exploration area.
② Matters necessary for the detailed criteria for approval of exploration under paragraph ① shall be prescribed and publicly notified by the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment. <Amended on October 1, 2025>

Article 18 (Submission of Exploration Results)
① A person approved for exploration under Article 13 (1) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as an “exploration right holder”) shall submit the exploration results under paragraph (6) of the same Article (hereinafter referred to as “exploration results”) to the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment, as prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Climate, Energy and Environment, by the end of the exploration period (or, if the submission period is extended under paragraph (7) of the same Article, no later than three months before the extended period ends). <Amended on October 1, 2025>
② “Grounds prescribed by Presidential Decree” in Article 13 (7) of the Act means any of the following grounds:
1. Exploration cannot begin or has been delayed due to a natural disaster or another unavoidable cause;
2. Extension of the period for submitting exploration results is deemed likely to lead to the discovery of a candidate carbon dioxide storage site under the former part of the provisions other than the subparagraphs of Article 14 (1) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as a “candidate storage site”);
3. Additional evaluation is necessary to verify the business feasibility of a discovered candidate storage site.
③ A person seeking an extension of the period for submitting exploration results under Article 13 (7) of the Act shall submit an application for such extension in the form prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Climate, Energy and Environment to the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment. <Amended on October 1, 2025>

Article 19 (Procedures for Revoking Approval of Exploration)
① If an exploration right holder falls under any subparagraph of Article 13 (5) of the Act, the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment may order corrective measures within a specified period. <Amended on October 1, 2025>
② If an exploration right holder who has received a corrective order under paragraph ① fails to make the correction within the specified period, the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment may revoke the exploration approval. <Amended on October 1, 2025>
③ If an exploration approval is revoked under Article 13 (5) of the Act, the exploration right holder shall promptly return the certificate of exploration approval under Article 16 (5) of this Decree to the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment. <Amended on October 1, 2025>

Article 20 (Criteria and Procedures for Selecting Candidate Storage Sites)
① “Documents prescribed by Presidential Decree” in Article 14 (2) 6 of the Act means the following documents:
1. Documents concerning the location of carbon dioxide injection wells at the candidate storage site;
2. A plan for securing local acceptance, including gathering opinions from residents of areas adjacent to the candidate storage site and public outreach.
② Upon receiving an application to select a candidate storage site under Article 14 (2) of the Act, the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment and the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries shall select a candidate storage site in consideration of the criteria for a storage site under Article 2 (6) of the Act. <Amended on October 1, 2025>
③ Detailed criteria and procedures for selecting candidate storage sites, other than those prescribed in paragraphs ① and ②, shall be jointly prescribed and publicly notified by the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment and the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries. <Amended on October 1, 2025>

Article 21 (Methods and Procedures for Public Announcement of Candidate Storage Sites)
① “Location of a candidate storage site and other matters prescribed by Presidential Decree” in Article 15 (1) of the Act means the following matters:
1. The name, location and area of the candidate storage site;
2. A land or seabed map showing the boundaries of the candidate storage site;
3. The carbon dioxide storage capacity of the candidate storage site;
4. The location of carbon dioxide injection wells at the candidate storage site.
② When selecting a candidate storage site under Article 15 (1) of the Act, the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment and the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries shall publicly announce that fact in the Official Gazette and on their respective agencies' websites. <Amended on October 1, 2025>

Article 22 (Revocation of Selection of Candidate Storage Sites)
① Before revoking the selection of a candidate storage site under Article 16 (1) of the Act, the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment and the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries may hear the opinions of the heads of relevant central administrative agencies. <Amended on October 1, 2025>
② When revoking the selection of a candidate storage site under Article 16 (1) of the Act, the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment and the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries shall publicly announce the following matters in the Official Gazette and on their respective agencies' websites: <Amended on October 1, 2025>
1. The name, location and area of the candidate storage site whose selection has been revoked;
2. A land or seabed map showing the boundaries of that candidate storage site;
3. The reason for revoking its selection.

Article 23 (Procedures for Closing Storage Sites)
① If a person permitted to conduct storage business under the main clause of Article 18 (1) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as a “storage business operator”) requests closure of a storage site, the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment may order its closure after consulting with the heads of relevant central administrative agencies under Article 17 (1) 2 of the Act; provided that this shall not apply if closure by the storage business operator is deemed detrimental to public safety or likely to cause harm. <Amended on October 1, 2025>
② Before ordering closure of a storage site under Article 17 (1) of the Act, the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment shall notify the relevant storage business operator in writing; provided that this shall not apply in an emergency. <Amended on October 1, 2025>
③ A storage business operator ordered to close a storage site under Article 17 (1) of the Act shall close it by a method prescribed and publicly notified by the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment, including sealing the storage site. <Amended on October 1, 2025>
④ Detailed matters necessary for the procedures and methods for closing storage sites, other than those prescribed in paragraphs ① through ③, shall be prescribed and publicly notified by the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment. <Amended on October 1, 2025>

Article 24 (Grounds for Closing Storage Sites) “Cases prescribed by Presidential Decree” in Article 17 (1) 4 of the Act means cases where a storage site needs to be used for another purpose, including national resource security.

Article 25 (Restoration of Installed Facilities)
① A storage business operator who has completed the closure of a storage site under Article 23 (3) shall restore structures, facilities and other equipment installed for storage business (hereinafter referred to as “structures and equipment”) in accordance with the following criteria:
1. Measures necessary to prevent secondary pollution and damage to ecosystems that may occur during restoration shall be taken;
2. The safety of workers shall be given priority;
3. Restoration shall be carried out according to the methods and procedures in the restoration plan under paragraph ②.
② A storage business operator shall submit to the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment a restoration plan covering the period needed for restoration under paragraph ①, the method of closing the carbon dioxide injection opening at the storage site, and the structures and equipment to be removed, and shall restore the site in accordance with that plan. <Amended on October 1, 2025>
③ If the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment determines that the restoration plan submitted under paragraph ② needs supplementation, the Minister may request the storage business operator to supplement it within an appropriate period. <Amended on October 1, 2025>
④ The Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment shall verify whether restoration has been completed in compliance with the criteria in the subparagraphs of paragraph ①. <Amended on October 1, 2025>
⑤ When a storage business operator completes restoration under Article 17 (5) of the Act, the operator shall submit to the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment an application for approval in the form prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Climate, Energy and Environment, accompanied by a report containing the following matters: <Amended on October 1, 2025>
1. Documents outlining the scope, location and size of the storage site being closed;
2. The management period after closure of the storage site;
3. The responsible organization and its person in charge;
4. The methods and procedures for closing the storage site;
5. Other matters prescribed and publicly notified by the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment as necessary for approval of completion of restoration.
⑥ The Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment may support all or part of the costs of restoring a storage site closed by a storage business operator under Article 17 (1) 3 or 4 of the Act. <Amended on October 1, 2025>

CHAPTER V PERMISSION FOR STORAGE BUSINESS

Article 26 (Procedures for Permission for Storage Business)
① A person seeking permission for storage business under the main clause of Article 18 (1) of the Act shall submit an application for permission for storage business in the form prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Climate, Energy and Environment to the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment, accompanied by the documents specified in the subparagraphs of Article 18 (3) of the Act. If the person has obtained permission from the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries under the proviso of Article 18 (1) of the Act, a copy of that permit shall also be attached. <Amended on October 1, 2025>
② Upon receiving an application for permission under paragraph ①, the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment shall verify the following matters through the shared use of administrative information under Article 36 (1) of the Electronic Government Act; provided that, if the applicant does not consent to verification of the certificate of business registration, the Minister shall require the applicant to attach that document: <Amended on October 1, 2025>
1. Certificate of business registration;
2. Certificate of registered corporate matters (only if the applicant is a corporation).
③ A storage business operator seeking to change a matter permitted under the main clause of Article 18 (1) of the Act shall submit an application for permission to change the storage business in the form prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Climate, Energy and Environment to the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment, accompanied by documents proving the change. <Amended on October 1, 2025>

Article 27 (Detailed Criteria for Permission for Storage Business)
① The detailed criteria for permission for storage business under Article 18 (4) of the Act are as follows: <Amended on October 1, 2025>
1. Measures to prevent disasters associated with commencing or changing storage business shall be established;
2. The plan for procuring financial resources needed to conduct storage business appropriately shall be specific and feasible;
3. The storage business operator's credit rating assessed by a credit information company under the Credit Information Use and Protection Act shall be at least the rating prescribed and publicly notified by the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment;
4. Relevant technical personnel shall be secured, including gas engineers, pipeline engineers and engineers for monitoring carbon dioxide leakage;
5. The plan for operating the storage site and related facilities shall be specific and feasible, and shall enable continuous and stable operation of the storage business.
② Detailed criteria necessary for permission for storage business, other than those prescribed in paragraph ①, shall be prescribed and publicly notified by the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment. <Amended on October 1, 2025>

Article 28 (Reporting Use of Storage Sites) A person intending to report the use of a storage site under Article 18 (5) of the Act shall submit a report on use of a storage site in the form prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Climate, Energy and Environment to the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment at least seven days before beginning to use the storage site. <Amended on October 1, 2025>

Article 29 (Reporting Succession to Business)
① A person intending to succeed to the status of a storage business operator under Article 20 (1) or (2) of the Act shall submit a report on succession to status in the form prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Climate, Energy and Environment to the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment, accompanied by documents proving the succession, within 30 days after the date of transfer, merger or acquisition. <Amended on October 1, 2025>
② Upon receiving a report on succession to status under paragraph ①, the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment shall verify the following matters through the shared use of administrative information under Article 36 (1) of the Electronic Government Act; provided that, if the reporting person does not consent to verification of the certificate of business registration, the Minister shall require that person to attach that document: <Amended on October 1, 2025>
1. Certificate of business registration;
2. Certificate of registered corporate matters (only if the reporting person is a corporation).

Article 30 (Reporting Commencement of Storage Business) A person intending to report the commencement of storage business under the main clause of Article 22 (1) of the Act shall submit a report on commencement of storage business in the form prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Climate, Energy and Environment to the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment at least 30 days before commencement. <Amended on October 1, 2025>

Article 31 (Applications for Permission to Suspend or Close Storage Business)
① A person seeking permission to suspend or close all or part of a storage business under the proviso of Article 22 (1) of the Act shall submit an application for permission in the form prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Climate, Energy and Environment to the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment, accompanied by the following documents: <Amended on October 1, 2025>
1. A copy of the resolution of the general meeting or board of directors concerning suspension or closure (only if the applicant is a corporation);
2. Documents outlining the scope, location and size of the storage site for which suspension or closure is sought;
3. A statement of reasons for suspension or closure;
4. A safety management plan for the storage site during the period of suspension or closure (only if the storage site is in operation).
② When deciding whether to permit suspension or closure of storage business under the proviso of Article 22 (1) of the Act, the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment shall consider the following matters: <Amended on October 1, 2025>
1. The effect of suspension or closure of storage business on surrounding ecosystems;
2. The possibility that the storage business operator will resume storage business (only in the case of suspension).
③ Upon receiving an application for permission to suspend or close storage business under paragraph ①, the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment shall notify the applicant whether permission is granted within two months after receiving the application. <Amended on October 1, 2025>

Article 32 (Imposition of Penalty Surcharges) The criteria for imposing a penalty surcharge on a storage business operator in lieu of an order to suspend business under Article 24 (1) of the Act shall be as specified in attached Table 2.

Article 33 (Procedures for Approval of Monitoring Plans)
① “Matters prescribed by Presidential Decree” in the former part of Article 25 (1) of the Act means the following matters: <Amended on October 1, 2025>
1. Methods for monitoring a storage site;
2. Changes in the amount of stored carbon dioxide;
3. Changes in storage conditions, including pressure changes at and around the storage site and physical changes in the Earth's crust;
4. Other matters that the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment deems necessary to ensure safety after closure of the storage site.
② “Period prescribed by Presidential Decree” in the latter part of Article 25 (1) of the Act means 15 years.
③ A storage business operator seeking approval of a monitoring plan under Article 25 (1) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as a “monitoring plan”) shall submit an application for approval in the form prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Climate, Energy and Environment to the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment, accompanied by the monitoring plan. <Amended on October 1, 2025>
④ “Heads of relevant central administrative agencies prescribed by Presidential Decree” in Article 25 (2) of the Act means the heads of the following central administrative agencies: <Amended on October 1, 2025>
1. The Minister of Trade, Industry and Resources;
2. The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport;
3. The Minister of Oceans and Fisheries;
4. The Administrator of the Korea Meteorological Administration.
⑤ After closure of a storage site, a storage business operator shall report the results of implementation of the monitoring plan for each year to the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment by the end of February of the following year. <Amended on October 1, 2025>
⑥ At the request of the head of a central administrative agency specified in a subparagraph of paragraph ④, the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment shall notify the head of the relevant agency of the results of implementation of the monitoring plan reported under paragraph ⑤. <Amended on October 1, 2025>

Article 34 (Exceptions to Prohibited Acts) “Grounds prescribed by Presidential Decree” in Article 26 (3) of the Act means the following grounds:
1. Where a storage site or monitoring facility is to be replaced due to malfunction or deterioration;
2. Where a storage site or monitoring facility is to be replaced by a safer and more efficient one through the application of new technology or similar means.

Article 35 (Condition of Carbon Dioxide to Be Stored)
① “Impurities prescribed by Presidential Decree” in Article 27 (1) of the Act means the following substances: <Amended on October 1, 2025>
1. Sulfur dioxide;
2. Nitric oxide;
3. Hydrogen sulfide;
4. Other substances prescribed and publicly notified by the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment.
② “Criteria prescribed by Presidential Decree” in Article 27 (2) of the Act means the following criteria:
1. The moisture content of carbon dioxide to be stored shall be kept at a level that does not corrode carbon dioxide transportation pipelines;
2. Carbon dioxide to be stored shall remain in a state other than multiphase flow during transport and storage.
③ The permissible tolerance for impurities in carbon dioxide to be stored and details of compression criteria concerning its condition, other than those prescribed in paragraphs ① and ②, shall be prescribed and publicly notified by the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment. <Amended on October 1, 2025>

Article 36 (Operation of Storage Sites)
① Under Article 28 (1) of the Act, a storage business operator shall take the following measures to prevent hazards at a storage site and related facilities and to prevent disasters:
1. Establishing and implementing a monitoring plan;
2. Applying for regular and ad hoc inspections under Article 28 (6) of the Act;
3. Inspecting, maintaining and repairing the storage site and related facilities.
② “Criteria prescribed by Presidential Decree” in Article 28 (2) of the Act means the criteria for the amount of natural leakage prescribed and publicly notified by the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment. <Amended on October 1, 2025>
③ If necessary to investigate the cause of leakage or other matters under Article 28 (3) of the Act, the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment may organize an investigation team. <Amended on October 1, 2025>
④ Matters necessary for the composition and operation of the investigation team and reporting of investigation results under paragraph ③ shall be prescribed and publicly notified by the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment. <Amended on October 1, 2025>
⑤ A storage business operator intending to temporarily suspend operation of a storage site under Article 28 (4) of the Act shall complete an application for approval of temporary suspension in the form prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Climate, Energy and Environment and submit it to the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment. <Amended on October 1, 2025>
⑥ Upon receiving an application under paragraph ⑤, the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment shall promptly decide whether to grant approval and notify the applicant. <Amended on October 1, 2025>
⑦ The period of temporary suspension of storage site operations under Article 28 (4) of the Act runs from the date on which notice of approval under paragraph ⑥ is received until the date on which an ad hoc inspection under Article 38 is completed.
⑧ During operation of the storage site, a storage business operator shall record and retain the following matters under Article 28 (5) of the Act:
1. The quantity and timing of carbon dioxide injection and the final quantity stored;
2. Pressure data for carbon dioxide injection wells and observation wells;
3. The results of implementing the monitoring plan following closure of the storage site.

Article 37 (Regular Inspections of Storage Sites)
① The criteria for regular and ad hoc inspections under Article 28 (6) of the Act are as follows:
1. Whether carbon dioxide is stored stably without leakage;
2. Whether equipment capable of measuring the pressure of carbon dioxide injection wells and observation wells has been installed;
3. Whether equipment capable of measuring changes in the storage site and the surrounding Earth's crust has been installed.
② The Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment shall conduct regular inspections under Article 28 (6) of the Act in the following categories: <Amended on October 1, 2025>
1. Initial inspection: within three months after a report on commencement of storage business under the main clause of Article 22 (1) of the Act is accepted;
2. Continuing inspection: within the three months preceding each one-year anniversary of the date of the initial inspection under subparagraph 1 or a change inspection under subparagraph 3;
3. Change inspection: within three months before resuming operation of the storage site following suspension or closure under the proviso of Article 22 (1) of the Act or temporary suspension of operation under Article 28 (4) of the Act.
③ Notwithstanding paragraph ②, if the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment determines that a storage business operator has difficulty undergoing a regular inspection at the prescribed time due to a natural disaster or another unavoidable cause, the Minister may defer the regular inspection. In such cases, the operator may undergo the inspection at another time determined in consultation with the institution or organization entrusted with regular inspection of storage sites under Article 69 (1) 4. <Amended on October 1, 2025>
④ Detailed criteria, items and methods for regular inspections and deferral of their conduct, other than those prescribed in paragraphs ① through ③, shall be prescribed and publicly notified by the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment. <Amended on October 1, 2025>

Article 38 (Ad Hoc Inspections of Storage Sites)
① The Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment may conduct an ad hoc inspection under Article 28 (6) of the Act if necessary to prevent accidents caused by carbon dioxide leakage or otherwise ensure the safety of a storage site. <Amended on October 1, 2025>
② Before conducting an ad hoc inspection, the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment shall notify the relevant storage business operator; provided that advance notice may be omitted in an urgent case, such as an anticipated accident, or if giving advance notice is deemed likely to defeat the purpose of the inspection due to destruction of evidence or similar circumstances. <Amended on October 1, 2025>
③ Detailed criteria, items and methods for ad hoc inspections, other than those prescribed in paragraphs ① and ②, shall be prescribed and publicly notified by the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment. <Amended on October 1, 2025>

CHAPTER VI DESIGNATION AND OPERATION OF CARBON DIOXIDE CAPTURE, STORAGE AND UTILIZATION CLUSTERS

Article 39 (Applications for Designation of Clusters)
① “Matters prescribed by Presidential Decree” in Article 29 (2) 5 of the Act means the following matters: <Amended on October 1, 2025>
1. The purpose of designating a carbon dioxide capture, storage and utilization cluster under the former part of Article 29 (1) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as a “cluster”) and its medium- and long-term development direction;
2. The status of the carbon dioxide capture, storage and utilization industry and infrastructure in the relevant area;
3. Matters concerning the installation of infrastructure for the cluster;
4. Measures to secure financial resources to foster the cluster;
5. The period of application of special regulatory exceptions to the cluster;
6. Other matters deemed necessary by the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment to foster the cluster.
② If a Special Metropolitan City Mayor, Metropolitan City Mayor, Special Self-Governing City Mayor, Do Governor or Special Self-Governing Province Governor (hereinafter referred to as a “Mayor/Do Governor”) seeks designation of a cluster under Article 29 (2) of the Act, he or she shall submit an application for designation in the form prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Climate, Energy and Environment to the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment, accompanied by a cluster development plan. <Amended on October 1, 2025>
③ When preparing a cluster development plan under paragraph ②, a Mayor/Do Governor shall hear the opinions of business operators, institutions and others associated with the proposed cluster.

Article 40 (Procedures for Designating Clusters)
① When the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment designates a cluster under the former part of Article 29 (4) of the Act, the Minister shall publicly notify the following matters in the Official Gazette and promptly inform the Mayor/Do Governor who applied for designation: <Amended on October 1, 2025>
1. The name, location and area of the cluster;
2. The purpose of designating the cluster;
3. The period and method of implementing the cluster project;
4. A topographical or cadastral map showing the cluster.
② Matters necessary for designating clusters, other than those prescribed in paragraph ①, shall be prescribed and publicly notified by the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment. <Amended on October 1, 2025>

Article 41 (Support for Clusters)
① “Matters prescribed by Presidential Decree” in Article 30 (1) 3 of the Act means the following matters:
1. Certification of carbon dioxide utilization technologies and products of enterprises in a cluster;
2. Joint research and development activities of universities, research institutes and enterprises in a cluster;
3. Promotion of exchanges and cooperation among universities, research institutes and enterprises in a cluster;
4. Development of sites and installation of medical, educational, housing and other convenience facilities for universities, research institutes and enterprises locating in a cluster.
② If the head of a relevant central administrative agency intends to support the costs necessary for projects specified in the subparagraphs of Article 30 (1) of the Act, he or she shall establish a cluster support project plan and give advance public notice of it on the agency's website.
③ A person seeking support under Article 30 (1) of the Act shall apply to the head of the relevant central administrative agency in accordance with the cluster support project plan under paragraph ②.
④ Upon receiving an application for support under paragraph ③, the head of the relevant central administrative agency shall notify the applicant of the decision on support within 30 days, unless there is a special reason otherwise.

Article 42 (Revocation of Designation of Clusters)
① “Grounds prescribed by Presidential Decree” in Article 31 (1) 3 of the Act means cases where operation of a cluster is no longer possible due to a natural disaster or another unavoidable cause.
② A Mayor/Do Governor intending to apply for revocation of the designation of a cluster under Article 31 (1) of the Act shall submit an application for revocation in the form prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Climate, Energy and Environment to the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment, accompanied by documents containing the following matters: <Amended on October 1, 2025>
1. The name and location of the cluster for which revocation is sought;
2. The reason for revoking designation of the cluster;
3. Problems arising from revocation of the designation and measures for managing them.
③ When the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment revokes designation of a cluster under Article 31 (1) through (3) of the Act, the Minister shall promptly inform the Mayor/Do Governor having jurisdiction over the cluster and publicly notify the following matters in the Official Gazette under Article 31 (4) of the Act: <Amended on October 1, 2025>
1. The name, location and area of the cluster;
2. The reason for revoking designation of the cluster;
3. The date of designation and the date on which revocation takes effect.
④ Upon receiving notice of revocation under paragraph ③, the competent Mayor/Do Governor shall post its contents on the agency's website for at least 14 days.

Article 43 (Reporting Operational Performance of Clusters)
① To evaluate the operation of a cluster under Article 32 (1) of the Act, the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment may request the competent Mayor/Do Governor to submit a report on the cluster's operational performance (hereinafter referred to as an “operational performance report” in this Article) by March 31 of the year following the year being evaluated. <Amended on October 1, 2025>
② An operational performance report shall include the following matters:
1. Operational performance of the cluster in the year being evaluated and its operational plan for the following year;
2. Results of using special regulatory exceptions and similar measures applicable to the cluster;
3. The contents and outcomes of projects conducted in the cluster;
4. The cluster's contribution to the regional economy;
5. Results of implementing improvement measures under Article 32 (3) of the Act.

Article 44 (Criteria for Evaluating Operational Performance of Clusters)
① The criteria for evaluating the operation of a cluster under Article 32 (1) of the Act are as follows: <Amended on October 1, 2025>
1. Whether the policy objectives and performance indicators for designation of the cluster have been achieved;
2. The extent to which operation of the cluster contributes to revitalizing the regional economy;
3. The extent of use of capture, storage and utilization technologies in the cluster;
4. Results and effects of using special regulatory exceptions and similar measures applicable to the cluster;
5. Other criteria determined by the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment as necessary to evaluate the cluster's operational performance.
② The Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment shall notify the competent Mayor/Do Governor of the results of a regular evaluation under Article 32 (1) of the Act by September 30 of the year following the year being evaluated; provided that, if unavoidable circumstances exist, that deadline may be extended by up to 60 days. <Amended on October 1, 2025>
③ The Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment shall post the results of an evaluation under Article 32 (1) of the Act on the Ministry's website. <Amended on October 1, 2025>

CHAPTER VII PROMOTION OF THE CAPTURE AND RELATED INDUSTRIES

Article 45 (Subject Matter and Criteria for Certification of Carbon Dioxide Utilization Technologies and Products)
① Under Article 34 (1) of the Act, the Minister of Science and ICT and the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment may certify the following technologies and products as technologies and products utilizing captured carbon dioxide (hereinafter referred to as “certification of carbon dioxide utilization technologies and products”): <Amended on October 1, 2025>
1. Technology that converts captured carbon dioxide into a useful resource, such as a chemical feedstock;
2. Products to which technology under subparagraph 1 is applied;
3. Other technologies or products jointly prescribed and publicly notified by the Minister of Science and ICT and the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment.
② The criteria for certification of carbon dioxide utilization technologies and products under Article 34 (1) of the Act are as follows:
1. The technology or product shall have an excellent effect in reducing greenhouse gases;
2. The technology or product shall be capable of commercialization.
③ Detailed criteria for certification of carbon dioxide utilization technologies and products under paragraph ② shall be jointly prescribed and publicly notified by the Minister of Science and ICT and the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment. <Amended on October 1, 2025>

Article 46 (Procedures for Certification of Carbon Dioxide Utilization Technologies and Products)
① A person seeking certification of a carbon dioxide utilization technology or product shall submit an application for certification in the form jointly prescribed and publicly notified by the Minister of Science and ICT and the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment to either Minister, accompanied by materials proving compliance with the certification criteria under Article 45 (2) and (3). <Amended on October 1, 2025>
② Upon receiving an application under paragraph ①, the Minister of Science and ICT and the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment shall determine whether the technology or product meets the certification criteria under Article 45 (2) and (3), and decide whether to grant certification. <Amended on October 1, 2025>
③ The Minister of Science and ICT and the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment may require an applicant for certification under paragraph ② to bear the costs necessary for certification. <Amended on October 1, 2025>
④ Certification shall be valid for three years and may be extended only once for a period not exceeding three years.
⑤ Matters necessary for certification of carbon dioxide utilization technologies and products, including certification procedures and methods, costs and extension of the validity period, other than those prescribed in paragraphs ① through ④, shall be jointly prescribed and publicly notified by the Minister of Science and ICT and the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment. <Amended on October 1, 2025>

Article 47 (Revocation of Certification of Carbon Dioxide Utilization Technologies and Products) When revoking certification of a technology or product under Article 34 (2) of the Act, the Minister of Science and ICT and the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment shall give public notice thereof on their respective agencies' websites. <Amended on October 1, 2025>

Article 48 (Certification Marks for Carbon Dioxide Utilization Technologies and Products) A certification mark under Article 34 (3) of the Act may be used, during the certification's validity period, only on the packaging, containers and promotional materials of a product using technology certified as a carbon dioxide utilization technology or a product certified as a carbon dioxide utilization product.

Article 49 (Procedures for Confirmation of Specialized Carbon Dioxide Utilization Enterprises)
① A person seeking confirmation as a specialized carbon dioxide utilization enterprise under Article 35 (1) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as a “specialized carbon dioxide utilization enterprise”) shall submit an application for confirmation in the form prescribed and publicly notified by the Minister of Science and ICT to that Minister, accompanied by the following documents:
1. Documents verifying management status, such as financial statements for the preceding business year;
2. Statements concerning the organization, facilities and specialized personnel;
3. Documents verifying total sales and the amount invested in research and development related to carbon dioxide utilization technologies;
4. Other documents prescribed and publicly notified by the Minister of Science and ICT as necessary to determine whether the requirements for a specialized carbon dioxide utilization enterprise are met.
② Upon receiving an application under paragraph ①, the Minister of Science and ICT shall verify the following matters through the shared use of administrative information under Article 36 (1) of the Electronic Government Act; provided that, if the applicant does not consent to verification of the certificate of business registration, the Minister shall require the applicant to attach that document:
1. Certificate of business registration;
2. Certificate of registered corporate matters (only if the applicant is a corporation).
③ Upon receiving an application for confirmation under paragraph ①, the Minister of Science and ICT shall review whether the applicant meets all requirements specified in the subparagraphs of Article 35 (1) of the Act and confirm the applicant as a specialized carbon dioxide utilization enterprise.
④ When confirming an applicant as a specialized carbon dioxide utilization enterprise under paragraph ③, the Minister of Science and ICT shall issue the applicant a certificate of confirmation under Article 35 (3) of the Act.
⑤ Confirmation as a specialized carbon dioxide utilization enterprise shall be valid for three years and may be extended only once for a period not exceeding three years.
⑥ Details necessary for confirmation of specialized carbon dioxide utilization enterprises, other than those prescribed in paragraphs ① through ⑤, shall be prescribed and publicly notified by the Minister of Science and ICT.

Article 50 (Requirements for Specialized Carbon Dioxide Utilization Enterprises)
① “Criteria prescribed by Presidential Decree” in Article 35 (1) 1 of the Act means the following criteria according to total sales:
1. For an enterprise whose total sales (meaning sales shown on the income statement for the business year preceding the business year in which the enterprise applies for confirmation under Article 35 (2) of the Act; hereinafter the same shall apply in this Article) are at least 100 billion won: at least 3 percent;
2. For an enterprise whose total sales are at least 30 billion won but less than 100 billion won: at least 5 percent or 3 billion won;
3. For an enterprise whose total sales are at least 10 billion won but less than 30 billion won: at least 7 percent or 1.5 billion won;
4. For an enterprise whose total sales are at least 2 billion won but less than 10 billion won: at least 10 percent or 700 million won;
5. For an enterprise whose total sales are less than 2 billion won: at least 15 percent or 200 million won.
② The amount invested in research and development related to carbon dioxide utilization technologies under Article 35 (1) 1 of the Act shall be the total of the following costs invested in the business year preceding the business year in which the enterprise applies for confirmation under Article 35 (2) of the Act:
1. Costs eligible for the tax credit for research and human resources development expenses under attached Table 6 of the Enforcement Decree of the Restriction of Special Taxation Act;
2. Costs equivalent to those under subparagraph 1, as prescribed and publicly notified by the Minister of Science and ICT.
③ “Requirements prescribed by Presidential Decree, including the level of technology and management capability” in Article 35 (1) 2 of the Act means the requirements prescribed and publicly notified by the Minister of Science and ICT for the following matters:
1. Status of certifications held for carbon dioxide utilization technologies and products;
2. Status of technical personnel related to carbon dioxide utilization;
3. Status of patents and dedicated research and development departments related to carbon dioxide utilization;
4. The enterprise's debt ratio;
5. Whether any administrative sanction has been imposed.

Article 51 (Support for Specialized Carbon Dioxide Utilization Enterprises)
① Under Article 35 (1) of the Act, the Minister of Science and ICT may provide a specialized carbon dioxide utilization enterprise with support for the following matters:
1. Development, demonstration and commercialization of excellent technologies related to carbon dioxide utilization;
2. Training of personnel in carbon dioxide utilization;
3. Certification of carbon dioxide utilization technologies and products;
4. Application of the special selection evaluation rule under the proviso of the part other than the subparagraphs of Article 10 (2) of the National Research and Development Innovation Act;
5. Other matters necessary to promote the growth and development of specialized carbon dioxide utilization enterprises.
② Before providing support under paragraph ①, the Minister of Science and ICT shall prepare a support plan specifying eligible recipients, qualifications and contents of support and give public notice of the plan on the Ministry's website for at least 30 days.
③ A person seeking support under paragraphs ① and ② shall apply to the Minister of Science and ICT in accordance with the support plan under paragraph ②.

Article 52 (Investigations for Confirmation of Specialized Carbon Dioxide Utilization Enterprises)
① The Minister of Science and ICT may conduct a documentary or on-site investigation upon receiving an application for confirmation as a specialized carbon dioxide utilization enterprise, or if it is necessary to determine whether an enterprise confirmed as such continues to meet the requirements in the subparagraphs of Article 35 (1) of the Act. To conduct an on-site investigation, the Minister shall notify the person subject to investigation of an investigation plan specifying its purpose, date, time and place at least three days before the investigation.
② Specific matters necessary for confirmation and follow-up management of specialized carbon dioxide utilization enterprises, other than those prescribed in paragraph ①, shall be prescribed and publicly notified by the Minister of Science and ICT.

Article 53 (Procedures for Revoking Confirmation of Specialized Carbon Dioxide Utilization Enterprises) When the Minister of Science and ICT revokes confirmation as a specialized carbon dioxide utilization enterprise under Article 35 (4) of the Act, the Minister shall give public notice thereof on the Ministry's website.

Article 54 (Support for Research on Technologies Related to Capture and Other Activities)
① When implementing any of the following projects, the head of a central administrative agency may support research, development and commercialization of technologies related to capture and other activities under Article 36 (1) of the Act: <Amended on October 1, 2025>
1. A national research and development project under Article 11 of the Framework Act on Science and Technology;
2. A specific research and development project under Article 14 of the Act on the Promotion of Basic Research and Support for Technology Development;
3. A technology development project under Article 8 of the Act on the Promotion of Development of Technology for Responding to Climate Change;
4. An industrial technology innovation project under subparagraph 7 of Article 2 of the Industrial Technology Innovation Promotion Act;
5. A research and development project for hydrogen-industry technologies under Article 30 (1) 2 of the Hydrogen Economy Promotion and Hydrogen Safety Management Act;
6. A research, development and technology evaluation project for new and renewable energy under Article 10 (2) of the Act on the Promotion of the Development, Use and Diffusion of New and Renewable Energy;
7. An environmental technology development project under Article 5 of the Environmental Technology and Industry Support Act;
8. A research and development project for marine and fisheries science and technology under Article 8 (1) of the Marine and Fisheries Science and Technology Promotion Act;
9. A technology development project under another Act that the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment determines in consultation with the heads of relevant central administrative agencies.
② Under Article 36 (2) of the Act, the head of a relevant central administrative agency may support public institutions, corporations, organizations and universities jointly researching technologies related to capture and other activities with costs necessary for joint use of research facilities and joint purchase of research equipment.

Article 55 (Applications for Approval of Demonstration Projects)
① A person intending to participate in a demonstration project using technologies related to capture and other activities under Article 37 (1) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as a “demonstration project”) shall submit a demonstration project plan containing the following matters to the head of the relevant central administrative agency:
1. The objectives, strategy and implementation structure of the demonstration project;
2. The technologies for capture and related activities to be applied in the demonstration project;
3. The site and financing necessary to implement the demonstration project;
4. Matters concerning the safety of implementing the demonstration project;
5. Measures to prevent accidents that may occur during implementation and compensate for personal injury and property damage.
② Under Article 37 (2) of the Act, the head of a relevant central administrative agency may approve a demonstration project plan submitted under paragraph ① if its contents meet all of the following criteria:
1. It shall be suitable for achieving the objectives of the demonstration project;
2. Its financing plan shall be appropriate and feasible;
3. It shall allow the demonstration project to proceed smoothly.

Article 56 (Procedures for Subsidies and Loans)
① The types and scope of detailed business categories in the industry concerning capture and related activities under the part other than the subparagraphs of Article 39 (1) of the Act shall be governed by the Special Classification of the CCUS Industry publicly notified by the Commissioner of the National Data Agency under Article 22 of the Statistics Act. <Amended on October 1, 2025>
② If the head of a relevant central administrative agency intends to subsidize or provide loans to related business operators to foster the industry concerning capture and related activities under Article 39 (1) of the Act, he or she shall first prepare a plan including a subsidy and loan execution plan and payment criteria and publicly announce it on the agency's website.
③ A person seeking a subsidy or loan under paragraph ② shall submit to the head of the relevant central administrative agency a business plan containing the following matters:
1. An overview and characteristics of the business;
2. The implementation structure and strategy;
3. An annual implementation plan for the business;
4. Measures for using the business and its expected effects;
5. Other matters concerning the business of capture and related activities prescribed and publicly notified by the head of the relevant central administrative agency.
④ The head of a relevant central administrative agency may evaluate a business plan submitted under paragraph ③ and subsidize or lend the necessary costs if its contents are appropriate.
⑤ The specific categories of subsidies or loans under Article 39 (1) of the Act and rates of support shall be determined by the head of the relevant central administrative agency in consultation with the Minister of Planning and Budget. <Amended on December 30, 2025>
⑥ “Costs prescribed by Presidential Decree” in Article 39 (1) 5 of the Act means the following costs:
1. Costs necessary to commercialize technologies for capture and related activities;
2. Costs necessary to apply for intellectual property rights concerning those technologies.

Article 57 (Funds Eligible to Invest in Capture and Related Businesses) “Funds prescribed by Presidential Decree” in Article 40 (3) of the Act means the Electric Power Industry Infrastructure Fund under Article 48 of the Electric Utility Act.

Article 58 (Designation of Institutions for Training Specialized Personnel)
① Under Article 41 (2) of the Act, the head of a relevant central administrative agency may designate any of the following institutions or organizations as an institution for training specialized personnel in technology development and industries related to capture and other activities (hereinafter referred to as a “specialized personnel training institution”): <Amended on October 1, 2025>
1. A university under Article 2 (1) of the Higher Education Act;
2. A government-funded research institute under the Act on the Establishment, Operation and Fostering of Government-Funded Research Institutes;
3. A government-funded research institute in the field of science and technology under the Act on the Establishment, Operation and Fostering of Government-Funded Research Institutes in the Fields of Science and Technology;
4. Another institution or organization providing education and training related to technology development and industries for capture and other activities, as publicly notified by the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment in consultation with the heads of relevant central administrative agencies.
② A person seeking designation under paragraph ① shall submit an application for designation in the form prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Climate, Energy and Environment to the head of the relevant central administrative agency, accompanied by the following documents: <Amended on October 1, 2025>
1. An education plan including a curriculum for training specialized personnel in carbon dioxide capture and related activities;
2. The status of teaching personnel, educational facilities and equipment necessary for that training;
3. A plan for procuring operating expenses.
③ Upon receiving an application under paragraph ②, the head of the relevant central administrative agency shall decide whether to designate the applicant in consideration of the following matters:
1. The systematic organization of the curriculum and its contents for training specialized personnel in carbon dioxide capture and related activities;
2. The expertise of education personnel and their capability to perform educational functions;
3. The suitability of facilities and equipment for the education;
4. Demand for education, including the number of specialized personnel and regional distribution of specialized personnel training institutions.
④ The head of a relevant central administrative agency may support all or part of the following costs of an institution designated under paragraph ①:
1. Lecture fees and allowances;
2. Costs of training materials and practical training equipment;
3. Other expenses necessary for training specialized personnel.

Article 59 (Designation of Specialized Institutions for Standardization Projects)
① The requirements for designation of a specialized institution or organization for technologies related to capture and other activities under Article 43 (3) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as a “specialized institution for standardization projects”) are as follows:
1. It shall be an institution or organization falling under one of the following items:
  (a) A public institution under Article 4 of the Act on the Management of Public Institutions;
  (b) A nonprofit corporation established under Article 32 of the Civil Act that performs work concerning business standardization of technologies related to capture and other activities;
  (c) A corporation or organization designated as a cooperative institution for developing industrial standards for those technologies under Article 5 (3) of the Industrial Standardization Act;
2. It shall have dedicated personnel, an organization and a work system necessary to perform standardization projects for those technologies.
② A person seeking designation as a specialized institution for standardization projects shall submit an application for designation in the form prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Climate, Energy and Environment to the head of the relevant central administrative agency, accompanied by documents proving compliance with the designation requirements under paragraph ①. <Amended on October 1, 2025>
③ Upon receiving an application under paragraph ②, the head of the relevant central administrative agency shall verify the following matters through the shared use of administrative information under Article 36 (1) of the Electronic Government Act; provided that, if the applicant does not consent to verification of the certificate of business registration, that head shall require the applicant to attach that document:
1. Certificate of business registration;
2. Certificate of registered corporate matters (only if the applicant is a corporation).
④ When designating a specialized institution for standardization projects under Article 43 (3) of the Act, the head of the relevant central administrative agency shall give public notice of the designation on the agency's website.
⑤ Detailed requirements for such designation, other than those prescribed in paragraphs ① through ④, shall be prescribed and publicly notified by the head of the relevant central administrative agency.

Article 60 (Support for Specialized Institutions for Standardization Projects) Under Article 43 (3) of the Act, the head of a relevant central administrative agency may provide a specialized institution for standardization projects with the following support:
1. Funding necessary to operate the institution's standardization projects;
2. Funding necessary to establish and operate shared research facilities for standardization research;
3. Other funding necessary for standardization research.

Article 61 (Criteria for Establishing the Promotion Center)
① “Institution or corporation meeting requirements prescribed by Presidential Decree, including specialized personnel and facilities” in Article 44 (1) of the Act means an institution or corporation meeting all of the following requirements: <Amended on October 1, 2025>
1. It shall be a corporation falling under either of the following items:
  (a) A government-funded research institute in the field of science and technology under the Act on the Establishment, Operation and Fostering of Government-Funded Research Institutes in the Fields of Science and Technology;
  (b) A nonprofit corporation established with permission from the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment under Article 32 of the Civil Act;
2. It shall have a dedicated organization, personnel and facilities necessary to perform the projects specified in the subparagraphs of Article 44 (2) of the Act.
② “Projects prescribed by Presidential Decree” in Article 44 (2) 7 of the Act means the following projects:
1. Support for establishing and using information systems related to capture and other activities;
2. Technological cooperation among industry, academia and research institutions, international cooperation and overseas expansion projects related to capture and other activities;
3. Other cooperative projects among institutions related to capture and other activities.
③ The head of the Carbon Dioxide Capture, Storage and Utilization Promotion Center under Article 44 (1) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as the “Promotion Center”) shall report the following matters to the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment by December 31 of each year: <Amended on October 1, 2025>
1. The contents of projects performed under Article 44 (2) of the Act;
2. Details of use of costs subsidized under Article 44 (4) of the Act.
④ Detailed criteria for establishing the Promotion Center, other than those prescribed in paragraphs ① through ③, shall be prescribed and publicly notified by the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment. <Amended on October 1, 2025>

Article 62 (Institutions from Which the Promotion Center May Request Personnel) “Universities, institutions or organizations prescribed by Presidential Decree” in Article 44 (3) of the Act means the following universities, institutions or organizations: <Amended on October 1, 2025>
1. A national or public school under Article 3 of the Higher Education Act (including a research institute attached to that school);
2. A government-funded research institute in the field of science and technology under the Act on the Establishment, Operation and Fostering of Government-Funded Research Institutes in the Fields of Science and Technology;
3. A specific research institute under the Specific Research Institutes Support Act;
4. Another institution or organization prescribed and publicly notified by the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment.

CHAPTER VIII SUPPLEMENTARY PROVISIONS

Article 63 (Designation of Institutions Dedicated to Public Monitoring)
① Under Article 45 (2) of the Act, the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment and the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries may each designate a person falling under any of the following subparagraphs as an institution dedicated to establishing and operating a system for public monitoring of capture and related activities (hereinafter referred to as “public monitoring” and a “dedicated institution,” respectively): <Amended on October 1, 2025>
1. A public institution under Article 4 of the Act on the Management of Public Institutions;
2. A government-funded research institute under the Act on the Establishment, Operation and Fostering of Government-Funded Research Institutes;
3. A government-funded research institute in the field of science and technology under the Act on the Establishment, Operation and Fostering of Government-Funded Research Institutes in the Fields of Science and Technology;
4. Another corporation or organization that the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment or the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries recognizes as having a dedicated organization, specialized personnel, expertise and facilities needed to perform public monitoring duties.
② Upon designating a dedicated institution under Article 45 (2) of the Act, the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment or the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries shall give public notice thereof on the relevant agency's website. <Amended on October 1, 2025>
③ Details necessary for the procedures and methods for designating dedicated institutions, other than those prescribed in paragraphs ① and ②, shall be separately prescribed and publicly notified by the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment and the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries. <Amended on October 1, 2025>

Article 64 (Operation of Institutions Dedicated to Public Monitoring)
① A dedicated institution shall perform the following duties: <Amended on October 1, 2025>
1. Verifying the appropriateness of the monitoring plan during carbon dioxide storage;
2. Verifying the final quantity of carbon dioxide stored at a storage site;
3. Verifying environmental effects in the storage area;
4. Verifying monitoring data concerning capture and related activities;
5. Other duties deemed necessary for efficient operation by the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment and the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries.
② The Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment or the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries may request the head of a dedicated institution to submit operating plans and other materials necessary to perform the duties specified in the subparagraphs of paragraph ①. <Amended on October 1, 2025>
③ Based on the results of an inspection of a dedicated institution's performance of duties, the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment or the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries may require its head to improve the performance of duties or take other measures. <Amended on October 1, 2025>

Article 65 (Submission of Reports on Public Monitoring Results) Under Article 45 (3) of the Act, the head of a dedicated institution shall prepare a report on public monitoring results containing the following matters and submit it to the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment or the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries by the end of February of each year: <Amended on October 1, 2025>
1. Information on operation of the storage site;
2. Details of verification under Article 64 (1) 1 through 5.

Article 66 (Disclosure of Monitoring Results) Under Article 46 (1) of the Act, the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment and the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries shall disclose the following matters concerning monitoring results obtained by storage business operators on their respective agencies' websites by March 31 of each year: <Amended on October 1, 2025>
1. Information on operation of the storage site;
2. The final quantity stored at the storage site;
3. Pressure data for the storage site.

Article 67 (Reports and Inspections) If the head of a relevant central administrative agency requests a report or submission of materials under Article 47 (1) of the Act, he or she shall give advance written notice of the deadline, method and contents of the report or submission; provided that this shall not apply in an emergency.

Article 68 (Orders to Storage Business Operators and Others to Take Measures)
① An order to take measures under Article 48 (1) of the Act shall be issued in writing specifying the following matters:
1. Details of failure to perform an obligation prescribed by the Act;
2. The contents of and grounds for the order;
3. The period for implementing the order.
② If the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment determines that an exploration right holder, pipeline installer and operator, storage business operator or dedicated institution has difficulty implementing an order within the period under paragraph ① 3 due to a natural disaster or another unavoidable cause, the Minister may extend the period only once, by no more than 30 days. <Amended on October 1, 2025>
③ Upon implementing an order to take measures under Article 48 (1) of the Act, a storage business operator or other addressee shall promptly report the results of implementation to the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment. <Amended on October 1, 2025>

Article 69 (Entrustment of Duties)
① Under Article 50 (2) of the Act, the Minister of Science and ICT and the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment may entrust any of the following duties within their respective jurisdictions to an institution or organization specified in the subparagraphs of paragraph ②: <Amended on October 1, 2025>
1. Investigation and analysis for establishing a master plan under Article 5 (1) of the Act and an implementation plan under Article 6 (1) of the Act;
2. Verification of compliance with safety management rules under Article 9 (3) of the Act;
3. Regular and ad hoc inspections of carbon dioxide transportation pipelines under Article 11 (1) of the Act;
4. Regular and ad hoc inspections of storage sites under Article 28 (6) of the Act;
5. Preliminary review of evaluations of clusters' operational performance under Article 32 of the Act;
6. Receipt and evaluation of applications for certification of carbon dioxide utilization technologies and products under Article 34 (1) of the Act;
7. Receipt and evaluation of applications for confirmation of specialized carbon dioxide utilization enterprises under Article 35 of the Act.
② An institution or organization eligible to be entrusted with duties under paragraph ① is as follows: <Amended on October 1, 2025>
1. The Promotion Center;
2. A government-funded research institute in the field of science and technology under the Act on the Establishment, Operation and Fostering of Government-Funded Research Institutes in the Fields of Science and Technology;
3. Another institution or organization publicly notified by the Minister of Science and ICT or the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment upon recognizing its expertise to perform the entrusted duties.
③ When entrusting duties under paragraph ①, the Minister of Science and ICT and the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment shall publicly notify the institution or organization entrusted and the contents of the entrusted duties. <Amended on October 1, 2025>

Article 70 (Processing Sensitive and Unique Identification Information)
① If unavoidable in performing any of the following duties, the Minister of Science and ICT (including a person entrusted with the Minister's duties under Article 69) may process materials containing a resident registration number or alien registration number under subparagraph 1 or 4 of Article 19 of the Enforcement Decree of the Personal Information Protection Act:
1. Duties concerning certification of carbon dioxide utilization technologies and products under Article 34 of the Act;
2. Duties concerning confirmation of specialized carbon dioxide utilization enterprises under Article 35 of the Act.
② If unavoidable in performing any of the following duties, the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment (including a person entrusted with the Minister's duties under Article 69) may process information constituting criminal-history data under Article 18 (2) of the Enforcement Decree of the Personal Information Protection Act (limited to the duties in subparagraphs 6 and 7), or materials containing a resident registration number or alien registration number under subparagraph 1 or 4 of Article 19 of that Decree: <Amended on October 1, 2025>
1. Duties concerning reports of plans to install carbon dioxide capture facilities under Article 7 of the Act;
2. Duties concerning approval of carbon dioxide transportation business under Article 8 of the Act;
3. Duties concerning reports of appointment of safety managers under Article 10 of the Act;
4. Duties concerning the conduct of safety inspections under Article 11 of the Act;
5. Duties concerning approval of exploration for storage sites under Article 13 of the Act;
6. Duties concerning permission for storage business under Article 18 of the Act;
7. Duties concerning reports of succession to the status of a storage business operator under Article 20 of the Act;
8. Duties concerning reports on commencement of storage business under Article 22 of the Act;
9. Duties concerning certification of carbon dioxide utilization technologies and products under Article 34 of the Act.

Article 71 (Review of Regulations)
① Based on January 1, 2025, the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment shall review the appropriateness of the following matters every three years (meaning by the day preceding the same base date in each third year) and take improvement or other measures: <Amended on October 1, 2025>
1. Criteria for approval of exploration under Article 17;
2. Criteria and procedures for selecting candidate storage sites under Article 20;
3. Requirements for specialized carbon dioxide utilization enterprises under Article 50.
② Based on January 1, 2025, the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment shall review the appropriateness of the following matters every five years (meaning by the day preceding the same base date in each fifth year) and take improvement or other measures: <Amended on October 1, 2025>
1. Restoration of installed facilities and equipment under Article 25;
2. Detailed criteria for permission for storage business under Article 27.

